# TODO - Diagnostics and launch at login

## Objective

Implement visible, reversible native launch-at-login and accept first-use
checks, service ownership, and tunnel lifecycle on the Mac.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS** when the teacher can install/launch the packaged service,
finish or repair first-use configuration, start and stop it predictably, and
opt in/out of launch at login. The local workspace and configured online MCP /
Cloudflare lifecycle must work without duplicate services, hidden persistence,
or lost settings; failures must be understandable and repairable.

Demonstrate working product flows and targeted startup/shutdown/recovery tests.
Use the owned OS lifecycle and leave Apple-specific trust, recipient-device
smoke tests, exotic permission cases, and compatibility polishing to the final
ongoing hardening task. An interrupted browser write must still never be
replayed merely because the service restarted.

## Implementation notes

The lightweight first-use check, persisted `diagnostics.json`, sanitized local
log, and manual rerun exist. It checks runtime, host, settings/storage, a tiny
native image round-trip, and secret-client presence. Fresh checks now validate
the configured local port and online prerequisites without persisting secret
values. Meaningful Keychain availability beyond client presence still requires
native acceptance without saving real credentials or mutating Blooket.

Shutdown/reload serialization and terminal stop were implemented and tested in
task 04. Preserve useful `cloudflared-unavailable`/timeout failure codes instead
of overwriting them with a generic close/offline state. Test disablement,
restart, early exit, missing executable/token, sleep/offline behavior, and
process cleanup.

Add visible stop/start and optional, reversible launch-at-login through ordinary
macOS facilities.

The packaged Cloudflare connector is resolved beside the runtime; missing-client
and connection-timeout status survive process close. Shutdown still closes the
gateway, local server, runtime record, and service lock when a connector stop
fails. Linux child processes keep only required host-session hints instead of
inheriting development credentials, including when opening the browser.

Explicit login startup is implemented below. Recipient-native assurance remains
in the final ongoing task, as requested by the owner.

This record alone owns host launch-at-login implementation. Task `blooket-17`
owns update preference integration and must reuse this lifecycle instead of
installing another startup agent. The existing `launchAtLogin` setting defaults
to false; bootstrap reports actual Service Management authorization separately
from this saved preference.

Native acceptance in task blooket-15 must enable login startup,
perform logout/login or restart,
observe exactly one owned service, disable it and confirm the next login does
not start it, and remove only owned persistence on uninstall. Updates preserve
the preference; registration, launch, and removal failures remain visible and
recoverable through ordinary supported macOS facilities.

Use Apple's `SMAppService.agent(plistName:)` for the bundled background helper
on the minimum macOS 13.5 target. Registration and removal must expose actual
service authorization status, including required user approval; the saved
preference alone does not prove an enabled login item. Registration may start
the helper immediately, so it must reuse the existing service lock and never
open a second UI or replay a remote write.

The native packaging work must compile/sign the Service Management helper and
include its launch-agent plist inside the application bundle. The package
builder now compiles an ARM64/macOS 13.5 login-agent helper and places its plist
under `Contents/Library/LaunchAgents`. `BundleProgram` points at the helper
inside `Contents/Resources/ServiceManagement`.

The helper invokes the existing packaged launcher with `--no-open`; it does not
register itself and the plist deliberately omits `KeepAlive`. Keep actual
signing and native login evidence explicit in task blooket-15.
Registration and reversible product controls are implemented by this functional
record; lack of a local Mac is not a completion gate.
This design follows Apple's
<!-- jig-ignore-next-line: Preserve canonical Apple API URLs. -->
[Service Management contract](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/servicemanagement/smappservice)
and its
<!-- jig-ignore-next-line: Preserve canonical Apple API URLs. -->
[registration semantics](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/servicemanagement/smappservice/register()).

## Evidence

On 2026-10-06, managed startup acquired a shared service lock and published a
private instance record. The launcher checks the live instance before reuse,
supports browser opening, status, and CSRF-protected stop, and releases the lock
on shutdown. Linux archive verification passed launch/reuse, native preparation,
the actual packaged CLI, foreign-origin rejection, and owned shutdown with
synthetic data. The real development service and `.env` were not changed.

On 2026-10-07, persisted first-use state gained an exact IR decoder and a
16-KB read ceiling. Startup refuses malformed schemas, unknown fields,
duplicate/missing checks, unsafe log paths, contradictory outcomes, symlink
targets, and non-files rather than returning unvalidated cached JSON.

A dedicated aggregate lock serializes fresh checks, cached reads, and explicit
reruns, preserving the storage probe and preventing another writer's log/state
replacement. Valid cached results are reused unchanged; a live owner returns a
bounded busy diagnostic and its lock is not stolen.

The explicit launcher `--diagnostics` option repairs corrupt cached state
without starting the background service or opening a browser. It returns
validated diagnostic JSON, refreshes the fixed local log, and fails with a
nonzero exit status when a check fails. Conflicting lifecycle options are
rejected; ordinary startup still fails closed on unreadable cached state.

Eight portable regression checks cover exact decoding, corrupted-state repair,
cached reuse, bounded/symbolic reads, occupied-lock ownership, and actual
launcher execution under a disposable data root. These checks do not prove
macOS launch-at-login, Keychain behavior, or recipient acceptance.

Strict TypeScript, roadmap validation, and all 723 portable tests passed at this
checkpoint. Jig reported only the existing external `JIG-RULE-GAP-001`; its
missing upstream evidence was not weakened or substituted.

On 2026-10-07, the ARM64 package definition gained the launch-agent packaging
foundation described above. Portable tests pin the owned label, plist location,
`BundleProgram`, `RunAtLoad`, absence of `KeepAlive`, and the helper's exact
`--no-open` launcher path. Native package verification now requires the helper
and plist and, on macOS, executes the helper before the ordinary launcher reuse
check. This repository session has no local Swift compiler, so compilation,
registration authorization, signing, and actual login startup remain unclaimed
until the ARM64 macOS path runs.

Fresh first-use diagnostics now reuse the same configured TCP-port resolver as
service startup. Fixed occupied ports fail explicitly, automatic mode may select
an available candidate, and a running service can identify its own persisted
port so a manual rerun does not mistake itself for a collision. When online MCP
is enabled, diagnostics require a usable gateway port plus the presence of the
owner verifier and Cloudflare tunnel token through the injected host-secret
store.

Missing or failed secret-store reads produce bounded codes; secret values never
enter diagnostic state or logs. Portable regressions cover fixed/automatic port
behavior, owned-port reruns, prerequisite ordering, and secret containment.

Online lifecycle regressions also pin disablement after a live connection, a
missing tunnel token before gateway creation, the gateway-port upper bound, and
cleanup of the active gateway/tunnel pair. Connector tests retain the useful
missing-executable and connection-timeout states after process close, classify a
clean early exit as offline, exclude the token from argv, and kill a child that
resists graceful shutdown. The production connection deadline remains 30
seconds; tests inject only a shorter deadline.

On 2026-10-08, the packaged main executable became a Swift wrapper using the
calling app's `SMAppService.agent(plistName:)`. Exact local commands inspect,
register, and unregister the owned bundled agent; ordinary launch never
registers persistence. Finder launch, the background helper, and canonical MCP
commands use direct processes and silent/piped streams without opening Terminal.

The owning local service exposes CSRF-protected login controls and stop.
Settings displays actual enabled, disabled, pending-approval, unavailable, or
unsupported status in English and Spanish. Registration and removal preserve
independent settings under the configuration lock, compensate failed preference
saves against observed OS state, and expose failed compensation for recovery.

The ordinary configuration endpoint refuses changed login preferences before
secret replacement or media operations. Registration is an explicit local
action; remote MCP has no installation or login-registration authority.
Startup and login helper reuse the existing service lock, so registration never
authorizes duplicate services or replays journaled Blooket writes.

Chrome exercised the actual local Settings controls against an explicitly
injected native adapter: enabling showed pending approval in both languages,
disabling showed disabled, and reloading retained the saved disabled preference.
This verifies the product UI and HTTP composition, not native registration.
The fixture used its own disposable data root and no account credentials.

Twenty-five targeted checks passed for login control, configuration, local HTTP,
packaging definition, and recovery. All 1,053 portable tests passed with four
isolated test processes, along with strict TypeScript and zero local Jig
diagnostics. Test concurrency is bounded because an unrestricted run exhausted
host capacity; no test or production deadline was removed or increased.

Apple's Service Management contract and registration/removal semantics were
checked through official documentation on 2026-10-08. Native Swift compilation,
Apple trust, Keychain, login/reboot, uninstall, and absence of visible Terminal
on an actual Mac remain explicitly unverified in task blooket-15. The owner's
instruction assigns these platform checks to P4; they do not keep this
implemented functional record open.
